<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neckar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neckar is a river in south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 360 km long, that rises near Villingen-Schwenningen at the edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> and flows north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tubingen">Tübingen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and Heilbronn to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mannheim">Mannheim</a>. Its valley was the heartland of Württemberg and is lined with vineyards, castles and old university towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neckar.jpg" alt="Neckar" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Berndt Fernow · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zuckerle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
